docs/grocery-router-prd.md:4:**Status of prototype:** Routing engine proven end-to-end on a real store floor plan (41/41 aisle markers auto-detected; optimal 11-item route computed and rendered; ~48% walking reduction vs average unordered list).
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:10:A mobile-first app that turns a grocery list into the **provably shortest walking path** through a specific store: entrance → every item → checkout. The user picks their store, enters their list, and gets a numbered route drawn on the store map that re-optimizes live as they check items off.
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:24:Shoppers with a list of 15–40 items waste significant time backtracking because lists are written in recall order, not store order. Store apps (H-E-B, Kroger) will show *one item's* aisle at a time, but no mainstream product sequences the *whole trip* into a shortest path. The cost: a typical unordered list at our pilot map walks ~760 m vs ~390 m optimal — roughly double the distance, plus the cognitive load of constant "where is this?" searching. The people who feel it most: parents shopping with kids, weekly bulk shoppers in large-format stores (H-E-B Plus is 100k+ sq ft), and anyone who hates the store.
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:38:- **Real-time indoor positioning** (blue-dot tracking). Requires beacons/dead-reckoning; the numbered-stop route works without it. Revisit at v3.
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:56:- As a contributor, I want basic aisle-order routing even before the map is processed so that the app is useful on day one.
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:64:- As a shopper, when I find an item in a different aisle than shown, I want a one-tap "wrong spot" report so that the data improves.
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:72:3. **Route** — one tap → optimal route rendered on the map: numbered stops, entrance → checkout, total distance/time.
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:98:   => LOCATION DB {store_id, product -> aisle/side/bay/pin, source, confidence, TTL}
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:104:   -> stop consolidation (items -> aisle-segment stops) §9.5
docs/grocery-router-prd.md:111:**Store selection & the store profile.** Everything is keyed by `(chain, store_id)` because aisle numbers are meaningless across stores. Selecting a store loads its profile: map asset, occupancy grid, anchor set, calibration, catalog/location cache pointers, provider config. If a store has **no map yet**, the app degrades to **serpentine mode** (§9.5, Level 0): items sorted by aisle number with alternating traversal — needs only aisle labels, no geometry — plus a prompt to contribute the map (Persona B).
